i>x</mi></mfrac></math>. Then, the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math> will be equal to :"}, "options": [{"id": "b46f09", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3612"}, {"id": "164ac2", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3613"}, {"id": "1c30ef", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3614"}, {"id": "f33656", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3615"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3616", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538323"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52a5"], "question": {"text": "The ratio of intensities at two points <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Q</mi></math> on the screen in a Young&#39;s double slit experiment where phase difference between two waves of same amplitude are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#960;</mi><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#960;</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, respectively are"}, "options": [{"id": "535ccc", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>3</mn></math>&nbsp;", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3617"}, {"id": "8288c6", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>3</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3618"}, {"id": "6e49ee", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3619"}, {"id": "785e78", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f361a"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f361b", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["6117b5e7478ae845c43c5ee8"], "concepts": ["6117b5e3478ae845c43c5dfa"], "question": {"text": "Visible light of wavelength <math><mn>6000</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> falls normally on a single slit and produces a diffraction pattern. It is found that the second diffraction minimum is at <math><msup><mrow><mn>60</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></mrow></msup></math> from the central maximum. If the first minimum is produced at <math><msub><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi></mi></math> then <math><msub><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> is close to"}, "options": [{"id": "256820", "text": "<math><msup><mrow><mn>20</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f361c"}, {"id": "685ec0", "text": "<math><msup><mrow><mn>30</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f361d"}, {"id": "c18f54", "text": "<math><msup><mrow><mn>25</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f361e"}, {"id": "f9d788", "text": "<math><msup><mrow><mn>45</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f361f"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3620", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b63"], "concepts": ["6117b5e3478ae845c43c5df1"], "question": {"text": "A system of three polarizers <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></math> is set up such that the pass axis of <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></math> is crossed with respect to that of <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> . The pass axis of <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></math> is inclined at <math><msup><mrow><mn>60</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></mrow></msup></math> to the pass axis of <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo>.</mo></math> When a beam of unpolarized light of intensity <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></mrow></msub></math> is incident on <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></math> the intensity of light transmitted by the three polarizers is <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math> . The ratio <math><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfenced></math> equals (nearly):"}, "options": [{"id": "2cd536", "text": "<math><mn>5.33</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3621"}, {"id": "603db4", "text": "<math><mn>16.00</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3622"}, {"id": "e1207e", "text": "<math><mn>1.80</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3623"}, {"id": "f5f945", "text": "<math><mn>10.67</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3624"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3625", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538323"], "concepts": ["6162fb516ed13040ec84d7f9"], "question": {"text": "In a Young's double slit experiment, the source is white light. One of the slits is covered by red filter and another by a green filter. In this case"}, "options": [{"id": "bb35c4", "text": "There shall be an interference pattern for red distinct from that for green.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3626"}, {"id": "e8ee69", "text": "There shall be no interference fringes.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3627"}, {"id": "6ba737", "text": "There shall be alternate interference fringes of red and green.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3628"}, {"id": "ac2f1e", "text": "There shall be an interference pattern, where each fringe's pattern center is green and outer edges is red.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3629"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f362a", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b63"], "concepts": ["60e567c3499d1e49588f993e"], "question": {"text": "When a polaroid sheet is rotated between two crossed polaroids then the transmitted light intensity will be maximum for a rotation of :"}, "options": [{"id": "104f21", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo>°</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f362b"}, {"id": "0a7c25", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn><mo>°</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f362c"}, {"id": "bb35e6", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>90</mn><mo>°</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f362d"}, {"id": "3d7ec8", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>45</mn><mo>°</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f362e"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f362f", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60e5678e499d1e49588f927c"], "concepts": ["6117b8bd67dbe2603c3e857f"], "question": {"text": "Two coherent sources of light interfere. The intensity ratio of two sources is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>4</mn></math>. For this interference pattern if the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></math> is equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#945;</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>&#946;</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>&#945;</mi><mi>&#946;</mi></mfrac></math> will be"}, "options": [{"id": "093b76", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3630"}, {"id": "3df42f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3631"}, {"id": "c613c3", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3632"}, {"id": "d7a8d6", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3633"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3634", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["6117b5e7478ae845c43c5ee8"], "concepts": ["6117b5e3478ae845c43c5dfa"], "question": {"text": "<p>Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R</p><p><strong>Assertion A :</strong> The beam of electrons shows wave nature and exhibit interference and diffraction.</p><p><strong>Reason R :</strong> Davisson Germer Experimentally verified the wave nature of electrons.</p><p>In the light of the above statements. Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "ee8ed3", "text": "A is correct but R is not correct", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3635"}, {"id": "998c19", "text": "A is not correct but R is correct", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3636"}, {"id": "ef1a6a", "text": "Both A and R are correct but R is Not the correct explanation of A", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3637"}, {"id": "218544", "text": "Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3638"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3639", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538323"], "concepts": ["60e2fb5666f9ce3f243b4ef4"], "question": {"text": "In the Young&#39;s double slit experiment, the distance between the slits varies in time as&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>d</mi><mfenced><mi>t</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>sin</mi><mi>&#969;</mi><mi>t</mi></math>; where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>&#969;</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math> are constants. The difference between the largest fringe width and the smallest fringe width obtained over time is given as:"}, "options": [{"id": "20e91e", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>D</mi><mfenced><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfenced></mrow><mfenced><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>-</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfenced></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f363a"}, {"id": "7643ff", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>D</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mfenced><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>-</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfenced></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f363b"}, {"id": "fb4882", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>D</mi></mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f363c"}, {"id": "12d0b0", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>D</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f363d"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f363e", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b63"], "concepts": ["6117b9d167dbe2603c3eacad"], "question": {"text": "<p>When unpolarized light is incident at an angle of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo>°</mo></math> on a transparent medium from air. The reflected ray is completely polarized. The angle of refraction in the medium is</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "d00ce5", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn><mo>°</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f363f"}, {"id": "5da415", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo>°</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3640"}, {"id": "bd390d", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>90</mn><mo>°</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3641"}, {"id": "ce2bb6", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>45</mn><mo>°</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3642"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3643", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60e5678e499d1e49588f927c"], "concepts": ["6117b8e067dbe2603c3e8a85"], "question": {"text": "Two light waves having the same wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi></math> in vacuum are in phase initially. Then the first wave travels a path <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> through a medium of refractive index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>while the second wave travels a path of length&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> through a medium of refractive index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>. After this the phase difference between the two waves is:"}, "options": [{"id": "8d0672", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mi>&#955;</mi></mfrac><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3644"}, {"id": "e27846", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mi>&#955;</mi></mfrac><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3645"}, {"id": "ddc291", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mi>&#955;</mi></mfrac><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3646"}, {"id": "388338", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mi>&#955;</mi></mfrac><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3647"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3648", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["6117b5e7478ae845c43c5ee8"], "concepts": ["6117b5e3478ae845c43c5dfa"], "question": {"text": "Which of the following phenomena can not be explained by wave theory of light?"}, "options": [{"id": "93318b", "text": "Compton effect", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3649"}, {"id": "d2c58a", "text": "Refraction of light", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f364a"}, {"id": "619504", "text": "Reflection of light", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f364b"}, {"id": "39e26a", "text": "Diffraction of light", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f364c"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f364d", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538323"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b470d"], "question": {"text": "In Young&#39;s double slit experiment, if the source of light changes from orange to blue then:"}, "options": [{"id": "e09fab", "text": "the central bright fringe will become a dark fringe.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f364e"}, {"id": "498046", "text": "the distance between consecutive fringes will decrease.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f364f"}, {"id": "2289bf", "text": "the distance between consecutive fringes will increase.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3650"}, {"id": "aa9c96", "text": "the intensity of the minima will increase.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3651"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef6c677dc36a88f3652", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b63"], "concepts": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b24"], "question": {"text": "Consider a tank made of glass (refractive index <math><mn>1.5</mn></math> ) with a thick bottom. It is filled with a liquid of refractive index <math><mi>&#956;</mi><mo>.</mo></math> A student finds that, irrespective of what the incident angle <math><mi>i</mi></math> (see figure) is for a beam of light entering the liquid, the light reflected from the liquid glass interface is never completely polarized. 